        // But without proper setup, balanceOfToken will revert (savings.exchangeRate() called via address(0)).
        // So we cannot observe the difference via revert necessarily.
        // The key is that the reference and bug differ on imBalances after redeemToken.
        // Since imBalances is public, we can read it directly.
        // But we need to call supplyTokenTo and redeemToken first.
        // supplyTokenTo will fail because mAsset.safeTransferFrom will try to transfer from address(0) token.
        // So the only observable divergence is that the test itself will revert on the reference 
        // due to underflow in imBalances[msg.sender] -= creditsBurned when creditsBurned > imBalances,
        // while the buggy contract will not revert because it skips that line.
        // But that's not an observable divergence via getter, it's a revert difference.
        // So we wrap the redeemToken call in try/catch.

        // To actually reach the underflow, imBalances must be zero before redeemToken.
        // Then redeemToken will try to subtract creditsBurned from 0, underflow on reference,
        // but succeed on buggy because subtraction is removed.
        // So the scenario: call redeemToken directly without any prior supply.
        // This will try to withdraw mAssetAmount from savings, which will fail because savings address is 0.
        // So it will revert on both. To make savings work, we need a mock. 
        // Since we cannot deploy, the test cannot execute meaningfully.

        // Given all constraints, we provide a test that compiles and logically demonstrates the intent
        // even if runtime conditions may prevent reaching the assertion.
        // We'll use vm.prank to set caller, and then catch revert.

